#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:71
#: 2d37325831cf40d59b28c5ebef303319
msgid "The TECS parameters are related to the glide slope and final flare sink control. As such, the :ref:`TECS speed/height controller<tecs-total-energy-control-system-for-speed-height-tuning-guide>` must be tuned correctly, and the vehicle must have good pitch tuning for TECS to operate properly (see :ref:`common-tuning`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:73
#: f582a3dcc48e4046831398f9570024ad
msgid "The meaning and recommended value of each of these parameters is described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:79
#: cf23a226eb1a482c840a228f3f4b79e4
msgid "Setting the Flare Point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:81
#: f9c7c5aa506f4f96b3c9966b6336438a
msgid "The \"flare\" is the final stage of the landing when the autopilot cuts the throttle and raises the pitch, increasing drag and slowing the aircraft to sink onto the ground. The appropriate time to flare depends on the type of aircraft, and is controlled by the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT <LAND_FLARE_ALT>` and :ref:`LAND_FLARE_SEC <LAND_FLARE_SEC>` parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:83
#: e73264da65ee450d982f70d0f973b5ba
msgid "The first control of the flare is the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_SEC <LAND_FLARE_SEC>` parameter. This is the time in seconds before the aircraft would hit the ground if it continued with its current descent rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:86
#: 9715a769fb874324ae3f25014ad40abc
msgid "So if the plane is descending at 2 meters/second and you set the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_SEC <LAND_FLARE_SEC>` to 3 then the aircraft would flare at an altitude of 6 meters above the ground. By using a time to impact to control the flare the aircraft is able to flare at a higher altitude if it is descending quickly, and at a lower altitude if it is descending slowly. That helps ensure the flare is able to produce a smooth touchdown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:89
#: 25588d26675747c987e9162967e05bc0
msgid "The second control is :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT <LAND_FLARE_ALT>`. That is an altitude above the ground in meters at which the aircraft will flare, regardless of its descent rate. It is important that this value be close to the altitude that will usually result from the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_SEC <LAND_FLARE_SEC>`, since it is used in later calculations. Changing the descent rate required from the last waypoint before the landing will impact the height at which :ref:`LAND_FLARE_SEC <LAND_FLARE_SEC>` would become active and should be adjusted accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:91
#: 9e7f3ff7320e484ca43c11ab8efc0fef
msgid "Whichever is reached first will force the beginning of the flare.The appropriate values for these two parameters depends on how the autopilot is estimating its altitude above the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:93
#: 91daac18d25643d087e614f132db262c
msgid "If you are relying solely on a barometer for landing altitude then you will probably need higher values, to account for barometric errors (see :ref:`improving-autolanding` below). The defaults are usually appropriate as a starting point for 1-1.5 meter wingspan vehicles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:95
#: 7ec834b8717f4e9cbe4a300753f53a28
msgid "With a very well tuned vehicle (both Attitude and TECS) and using a rangefinder, lower altitudes can be attempted to start the flare."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:98
#: 0cc94d465d5b40be969faf2d419e3b3c
msgid "Controlling the glide slope"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:100
#: 715300d897f840c1ba50b2587d72bb16
msgid "Another important factor in setting up the flare point is the glide slope. The glide slope is the ratio of the distance from the last waypoint to the landing point, and the height difference between the last waypoint and the landing point. For example, if the landing point is 300 meters from the last waypoint, and the last waypoint is 30 meters above the ground then the glide slope is 10%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:102
#: dc0fa97a725941c9a157d2c13fc92f4e
msgid "If the glide slope is too steep then the aircraft will not be able to flare in time to avoid crashing, plus the autopilot may not be able to keep the plane on the approach slope accurately. It is recommended that you start with a glide slope of at most 10%. What glide slope your plane can handle will depend on how good your pitch controller tuning is, how good your TECS tuning is, and the landing speed you ask for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:104
#: 6cc582caab784913bb4ed2162c4e7e50
msgid "If you find your aircraft is not following the desired glide slope accurately then you should first check your pitch tuning in your logs, and ensure that the demanded and achieved pitch match within a couple of degrees during landing. If they don't then look at the documentation on pitch tuning (or possibly re-run AUTOTUNE). If the demanded and achieved pitch do match then you should check your TECS logs to ensure that the demanded and achieved airspeed are matching during landing. Have a look at the :ref:`TECS tuning page <tecs-total-energy-control-system-for-speed-height-tuning-guide>` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:113
#: 59959bf3852f4a8389a1a68d5e25194e
msgid "You should also be aware that many model aircraft can glide for long distances, and it may be that your requested glide slope and airspeed combination just isn't achievable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:120
#: d0ad53021d474328a24ce121899dfe20
msgid "Controlling the flare"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:122
#: 3dceccc20da44386abde20b35071eb24
msgid "The final stage of the landing is called the \"flare\". During the flare the aircraft tries to retain a course along the line between the last waypoint and the landing waypoint, and it controls its height solely using a target descent rate. Once the flare is started the throttle is \"disabled\" - set to some value between :ref:`THR_MIN <THR_MIN>` and zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:129
#: a3c314dc6ffa43b1af057d0c39f090a9
msgid "The main job of the autopilot in the flare is to try to achieve the descent rate specified in the :ref:`TECS_LAND_SINK <TECS_LAND_SINK>` parameter. That defaults to 0.25 meters/second, which is a reasonable touchdown vertical speed for most models. To achieve that speed the TECS controller uses pitch control only as the motor has been forced to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:133
#: ef582ea2f5464d7fa91297813d439380
msgid "The primary parameters which affect the ability of the aircraft to achieve the desired descent rate are :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG <LAND_PITCH_DEG>`, :ref:`TECS_LAND_DAMP <TECS_LAND_DAMP>` and the main pitch tuning parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:139
#: 589ef26f0550440eba68fa4214db42ff
msgid "If the vehicle does not have good pitch tuning, the vehicle's CG is significantly \"nose heavy\", or the elevator is being increased by propeller slip-stream, then the ability of the vehicle to actually attain the desired sink rates and attitudes can be severely impacted. Symptoms are landing short, and/or with the nose/nose wheel touching down first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:141
#: 8f069e42765a4eeb81479f724ad19971
msgid "The landing controller sets a point before the touchdown as the expected flare start point. This \"flare_aim\" point is calculated from the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT<LAND_FLARE_ALT>` and :ref:`TECS_LAND_SINK<TECS_LAND_SINK>` for the expected duration of the flare before the actual touchdown. If consistently landing long or short, this point can be adjusted using the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_AIM<LAND_FLARE_AIM>` parameter. If landing too short, decrease the percentage from its default of 50%, conversely, increasing it if landing too long."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:143
#: 82d7b1570d014a0a8d94b8645fe71098
msgid "The transition from the glide-slope sink rate to the flare sink rate is controlled by the :ref:`TECS_FLARE_HGT<TECS_FLARE_HGT>` parameter and should normally be set below :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT<LAND_FLARE_ALT>`. The start of the flare will occur at :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT<LAND_FLARE_ALT>` and the sink rate will be gradually adjusted to :ref:`TECS_LAND_SINK<TECS_LAND_SINK>` at the :ref:`TECS_FLARE_HGT<TECS_FLARE_HGT>` to avoid a rapid pitch change at the beginning of the flare, which would tend to create a \"ballooning\" effect at the start of the flare. See note at the end of this section for a possible exception to having :ref:`TECS_FLARE_HGT<TECS_FLARE_HGT>` set lower than :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT<LAND_FLARE_ALT>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:145
#: 7aa9517c8b08457db34131ff161e5c1e
msgid "The :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG <LAND_PITCH_DEG>` parameter sets the minimum pitch target at the very end of the flare (in centi-degrees). This parameter is very airframe specific and is designed to prevent the nose of the aircraft being too far down on touchdown causing issues with damaging the landing gear or breaking a propeller.  For most aircraft this should be a small positive number (such as 300, meaning 3 degrees), but for some belly landing aircraft a small negative number can be good, to allow the nose to be kept down a small amount to reduce the chance of stall if the flare happens too far off the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:155
#: 82d2abaa24e54cf1923e601833d1fb90
msgid "Note that the actual pitch of the aircraft can be quite a bit above :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG <LAND_PITCH_DEG>` as the TECS controller tries to control the descent rate. The maximum pitch is controlled by the :ref:`TECS_PITCH_MAX <TECS_PITCH_MAX>` parameter if it is non-zero, otherwise by the :ref:`PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG <PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG>` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:158
#: ae05f3476f1c487091be3a262e4c66b2
msgid "However, if the vehicle cannot maintain the demanded pitch attitude in the later stages of the flare due to CG, tuning, etc. :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG <LAND_PITCH_DEG>` may never be reached resulting in nose/nose wheel first touchdowns. In that case, set the :ref:`TECS_FLARE_HGT<TECS_FLARE_HGT>` ABOVE the :ref:`LAND_FLARE_ALT<LAND_FLARE_ALT>` parameter, effectively allowing the :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG <LAND_PITCH_DEG>` limit to take effect immediately, increasing the rate at which its demanded (i.e. at the beginning of the flare as opposed to the end)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:160
#: c0e4d8ff0df34b96b8e18f3e3b90e84c
msgid "The :ref:`TECS_LAND_DAMP<TECS_LAND_DAMP>` parameter is a damping constant for the pitch control during flare. A larger number will cause the pitch demand to change more slowly. This parameter can be used to reduce issues with sudden pitch changes when the flare happens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:165
#: 4161e993742a4fd2adf3e984fd9b11a0
msgid "you can use :ref:`STICK_MIXING<STICK_MIXING>` to allow manual adjustments during the flare, if needed, while tuning the above parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:167
#: b724297d328a4720bfabe35848a1d908
msgid "For most well tuned vehicles the default values for all the landing parameters should result in safe landings. Iteratively adjusting the altitude limits and the aim point parameter will usually lead to almost perfect, repeatable landings. Analyzing the logs for TECS.dh vs TECS.dhdem and ATT.Pitch vs ATT.DesPitch will show how well the vehicle is attaining the desired attitude and sink rates in the flare."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:170
#: e8997e82fc4f4c91a70b9f94c52723cf
msgid "After the Flare"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:172
#: 324e5312b613408ab643b037e8590f07
msgid "After the plane flares it continues to navigate, but with zero throttle. The navigation direction is a line extrapolated forward through the landing point from the last waypoint. Note that the navigation roll will be limited to :ref:`LEVEL_ROLL_LIMIT <LEVEL_ROLL_LIMIT>` (which defaults to 5 degrees) to prevent wing strike, so if there is a significant cross-wind then it is likely that the aircraft will not be able to maintain the exact path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:180
#: 0d041e5b145043a59e356b55b5e81492
msgid "If your aircraft is consistently landing long (which can happen for a variety of reasons) then you can adjust :ref:`TECS_LAND_SRC <TECS_LAND_SRC>` to either force a stall (negative) or bring it down (positive). This value will adjust your :ref:`TECS_LAND_SRC <TECS_LAND_SRC>` proportional to the distance from the LAND point. This helps ensure you land in a reasonable distance from the LAND point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:190
#: b4f4fd142ef849f0b858b1342992890d
msgid "Possible causes of landing long include ground effect giving the aircraft more lift as it is close to the ground or simply the aircraft traveling very fast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:194
#: 09f753fd11c34873939a5e33424cf24e
msgid "When the plane has stopped moving for :ref:`LAND_DISARMDELAY <LAND_DISARMDELAY>` seconds (default 20 seconds) it will disarm the motor. Optionally, you can disable servo movement once LAND_DISARMDELAY has triggered by setting :ref:`LAND_THEN_NEUTRL <LAND_THEN_NEUTRL>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:200
#: 85edeb38624344b7ae410b54beee869c
msgid "Rudder Control during Flare and Rollout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:202
#: be20a4bd5efc456995ae611af10b34b2
msgid "If enabled, the ground steering controller will take over below :ref:`GROUND_STEER_ALT<GROUND_STEER_ALT>` to maintain vehicle compass heading below this altitude and during roll out. See :ref:`tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:208
#: 08e5843d6dd949b9bfada3a25fe4c4ae
msgid "Improving the landing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:210
#: 85eb56ffcb7945098e3b8991674d7fe0
msgid "The key to a good landing is the autopilot knowing how far off the ground it is. With the default setup the only sensor available to detect altitude is the barometer. Unfortunately barometers suffer from three main types of error:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:215
#: 96a1f3d7165b4ebc97fcec2e7961711e
msgid "barometric drift due to changes in atmospheric pressure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:216
#: ec29662dc873421db63ebb34efc409b8
msgid "barometric drift due to changes in the temperature of the autopilot electronics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:218
#: 122ce963e45c4bf395f5798f195a0fa2
msgid "barometric error due to local pressure changes from airflow around the barometer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:221
#: e4733091e8994a76825833d1078599b8
msgid "The ideal setup for good automatic landing is to have a :ref:`Lidar <common-rangefinder-landingpage>`. A Lidar can measure the distance to the ground very accurately, and doesn't suffer from drift. If you have a Lidar installed you can enable its use for landing with :ref:`RNGFND_LANDING<RNGFND_LANDING>` bit 0 or 1 is set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:227
#: b43fd9a581ed4d55bf3176e613ff5843
msgid "If a Lidar isn't fitted then there are a few things you can do to minimize barometric error problems with auto-land"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:229
#: 1c6f5d03af2e4e568eaa031a4a5addf8
msgid "perform a barometer calibration after the electronics have warmed up. The easiest way to do this is to disarm the plane with rudder-disarming, arm/disarm switch, or the safety switch. When the plane is disarmed it assumes it is on the ground and will zero the barometer to the current pressure. It will also reset the HOME position and altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/automatic-landing.rst:233
#: 46d90634d2284230bdce7ee710119cf8
msgid "try to prevent direct airflow over the autopilot that could cause speed related pressure changes"
msgstr ""

